WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.589
This video is sponsored by Brilliant.org and
it’s the second video in a mini series about

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:12.710
Linear Algebra. Click here and watch the first
one on vectors and bases for vector spaces.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:18.090
This video is about Matrices. When I first
met a matrix it was in a textbook question.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.949
It asked me to put a bunch of numbers to do
with a bakery’s sales in an array. I was

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:28.200
very confused. It took me a long long time
to really understand that matrices have really

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.619
nothing to do with bakeries and that just
the same way that writing a vector as a column

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:38.480
of numbers is just a shorthand, writing a
matrix as an array of numbers is a shorthand

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:39.820
too.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:49.410
This is what Matrices really are: they are
linear transformations.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.751
Ok so let me explain. Remember vectors from
last time? Well imagine you have a some vector

00:00:54.751 --> 00:01:01.910
and want to transform it into some other vector
instead. In fact say you have some whole vector

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.820
space, let’s say all the vectors in 2D space,
and you want to transform them into some vectors

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:14.140
in another vector space or the same space,
for example vectors in 3D space. Then a transformation

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.140
is just something that eats a vector from
your initial space and spits out a vector

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.860
in your new space.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.480
A Matrix is a transform. But a very specific
type called a linear transform, which is something

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.520
that respects linear combinations. Let’s
say that I have two vectors. I’m going to

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.820
apply this matrix to them each and that will
transform them in some way. Then, I can make

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:49.659
a linear combination of the result. But what
if I decided to do this linear combination

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:57.350
first, and then put the resulting vector into
the matrix.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:03.200
A linear transformation is one where these
to processes result in the same final vector.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.700
In other words, you can first put your two
vectors into the matrix, and then take a particular

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.950
linear combination of them or you can take
the linear combination of them first, then

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.890
apply the matrix. Either way you will get
the same result.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:21.310
To see if you’ve understood, I want you
to figure out if the following transformations

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.920
are linear transformations or not.
The first is a transformation that takes a

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.200
2d vector and then just multiplies its length
by 3.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.580
The second is like this. First draw a line
through the origin. Then take any vector in

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:43.001
2d, say this one, and draw a perpendicular
from that line to the tip of the vector. The

00:02:43.001 --> 00:02:46.290
new vector is on the line.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:51.120
So are these two linear transformations? Think
about the answer and then put it in the poll

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.390
in the corner. Remember that committing to
an answer like this helps you learn.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:06.110
Ok, so the answer might surprise you but the
answer is that they’re both linear. To see

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:12.400
this for the first one, take any two vectors
and apply the transformation to them then

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.760
make a linear combination of them. This is
clearly the same as if you made the linear

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:24.770
combination and then multiplied the result
by 3. The second one is a bit tricker, but

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:30.530
the easiest way to see it is, first draw two
vectors. Then notice that these vectors are

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:40.120
equal to these two perpendicular vectors added
together.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:46.620
When you apply the transform to them, what
it does is it cuts off this bit. Then if you

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:53.650
add them you get this. But if you’d instead
added the original vectors first, it would

00:03:53.650 --> 00:04:03.730
be like adding all these 4 bits first, which
you can group up like this. Now if we apply

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:11.020
the transform it cuts off this bit, and see,
we have the same thing in both cases. Kind

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.580
of neat right?

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:20.940
But here’s a really nice thing about linear
transforms. Imagine you have a linear transform

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:26.430
from some initial space, to some other space
and you have an arbitrary basis for your initial

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:37.240
space 
Now let’s suppose that we know exactly what

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:43.310
the linear transform does to the basis vectors.
This is in fact enough to figure out what

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:49.610
it does to the entire space. To see why, say
you have some other vector in this initial

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:55.500
space. You can write it as a linear combo
of basis vectors- in 2d remember you do this

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:17.970
by using 

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.020
a grid. Ok, once you’ve finally got that
linear combination if you apply the linear

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.630
transformation to it, you can see by the linearity
that you only need to know what happens to

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:32.110
the basis to compute this thing.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:39.230
So now we get to writing a matrix as an array.
What you’ll see is, this first column represents

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:46.190
what happens to your first basis vector, the
second what happens to your second etc. Remember

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:52.120
how to write vectors as columns in some basis?
It’s a shorthand for writing out this linear

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:57.450
combination of basis vectors. Well then clearly
the basis vectors are written like this.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:03.660
Say I apply my linear transform to the first
basis element and I get this column vector,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.289
and I do this for each basis vector. As we
said before this is everything I need to know

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:15.350
about this linear transform, so I want to
store this information compactly, so I store

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:22.400
it in a matrix like this. And that is really
all a matrix is. It’s shorthand, where,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.840
just like in the case of vectors written as
columns, the basis is hidden. But the basis

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.590
is super important, so this array of numbers
only represents a linear transformation as

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:38.110
long as you’re clear what the basis is.

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:43.910
Now I can explain what it means to ‘multiply’
a matrix by a vector. It’s not about multiplication

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:49.729
at all. All it means is you apply the linear
transformation that this matrix represents

00:06:49.729 --> 00:07:10.131
to the vector this represents. As we saw,
this is just equal to this big sum.. Which

00:07:10.131 --> 00:07:19.630
you might recognise as the rule for multiplying
matrices and vectors.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:25.050
To see if you’ve got this, see if you can
come up with a matrix for the following linear

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:30.449
transformation: It takes the first basis vector
and does nothing to it, and takes the second

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:36.050
basis vector and deletes it. Another way of
thinking of that is, it takes the second basis

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:46.289
vector to the zero vector- the vector with
no length. Pause the video now and try to

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:59.490
come up with the matrix in this basis. Hopefully
you’ve had a think. The answer is this.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:04.169
By the way, your matrices definitely don’t
need to be square. The dimensions of the matrix

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:09.250
tell you about the dimension of the vectors
it eats vs the dimension of the vectors it

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.990
spits out.
In this example, this matrix takes 3d vectors,

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:20.710
and gives back 2d ones. Then it’s shape
has to be this, because it has 3 rows, representing

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:26.770
what the matrix does to the 3 basis vectors
of the initial space, and 2 columns because

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:31.509
each basis vector becomes a 2d vector.

00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:35.829
If you’ve following everything so far, then
matrix multiplication is going to be really

00:08:35.829 --> 00:08:41.149
simple to explain. You know how when you first
meet matrix multiplication, they throw this

00:08:41.149 --> 00:08:46.959
crazy formula at you. Then they expect you
to act shocked when you find out, if you call

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:53.269
this insane thing multiplication, then A times
B doesn’t equal B times A. But there is

00:08:53.269 --> 00:08:59.139
a much much easier way to understand matrix
multiplication. Let’s look at an example

00:08:59.139 --> 00:09:00.809
first.

00:09:00.809 --> 00:09:04.800
Imagine if you want to transform some vectors.
First you want to rotate them by 90 degrees

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:13.220
in the plane. Then you want to project them
onto this line like we did earlier. Both of

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:19.399
these are linear, so call the matrix representing
these transformations in some basis A and

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:30.569
B. Here’s a question. Is it the same thing
to do the projection first, then the rotation?

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:34.820
I can tell you, it’s not, but I want you
to pause the video now and think of a vector

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.310
that would come out different if you do the
projection first or if you do it second. If

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:48.619
you could think of one, say so in the poll
here.

00:09:48.619 --> 00:09:59.639
Ok, so there are many options but here’s
a vector that works: If the vector starts

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:06.899
on the line, and you apply A it gets rotated
to here, then when you apply B to the result

00:10:06.899 --> 00:10:13.850
you project it down, but in this case, nothing
happens to it. But if you did it in the other

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:17.850
order when you apply B it immediately becomes
the zero vector, and then when you apply A

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:25.119
nothing else happens. So clearly doing A then
B is different to doing B then A

00:10:25.119 --> 00:10:31.139
So here’s the big secret: When we write
B A, the linear transformation that we’re

00:10:31.139 --> 00:10:37.689
talking about is the one where you do A first
and then B. That’s all it means. And as

00:10:37.689 --> 00:10:42.420
we’ve just seen, order matters, so this
is different from the linear transformation

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:49.580
A B, since in that case you swap the order.
So the statement AB doesn’t equal BA is

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:54.759
actually blindingly obvious, once you understand
the meaning of these things and really shouldn’t

00:10:54.759 --> 00:10:59.100
be presented as a startling fact.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:04.509
So why does this lovely idea of composing
maps lead to the awfulness that is the matrix

00:11:04.509 --> 00:11:09.290
multiplication formula? Well actually, once
you understand it, the formula isn’t really

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:17.199
ugly at all. Let me show you what I means
for the case when A and B are 2x2 matrices.

00:11:17.199 --> 00:11:22.431
How would we try and write AB as a matrix
in a particular basis? Well, let’s follow

00:11:22.431 --> 00:11:28.480
our own rules. We need to find out what AB
does to the each of the basis vectors. Let’s

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:34.730
do the first one. Well, when we apply B, we
get this first column of B, by definition.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:42.869
But then when we apply A, we get b_11 times
the first column of A plus b_21 times the

00:11:42.869 --> 00:11:52.829
second column. This result is our first column
of AB. Do the same for the second basis vector,

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:59.399
and here is the formula. See? Matrices aren’t
so painful, after all.

00:11:59.399 --> 00:12:04.199
OK, It’s homework time!

00:12:04.199 --> 00:12:09.689
First, Not all sized matrices can be multiplied
together. Think about it in terms of them

00:12:09.689 --> 00:12:15.429
representing transformations from one space
to another, and figure out which size matrices

00:12:15.429 --> 00:12:19.049
can be multiplied and explain why in the comments.

00:12:19.049 --> 00:12:24.800
Second, and this is a multiple choice one,
consider a transformation that takes a 3d

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:33.609
vector, and adds some fixed vector k to it.
Say k is the vector (7 3 3). Is this a linear

00:12:33.609 --> 00:12:35.379
transformation or not?

00:12:35.379 --> 00:12:42.239
Third, imagine you have a matrix A that multiplies
the first basis vector by 2, and the second

00:12:42.239 --> 00:12:49.009
basis vector by 6. How do you write A in this
basis?

00:12:49.009 --> 00:12:52.730
As you will have noticed, these questions
are from Brilliant.org who are sponsoring

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:58.560
this video. I said this last time, but I think
linear algebra is just a topic you can’t

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:02.830
understand without doing examples. If you
try and understand the topic just through

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:07.019
learning a bunch of formulas or theorems you
just won’t be able to get all the subtleties

00:13:07.019 --> 00:13:11.069
involved. I found that when I went through
Brilliant’s course, which has really well

00:13:11.069 --> 00:13:15.470
designed examples, I remembered a bunch of
little tricky points that I’d forgotten

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:19.220
about, and it got me really comfortable with
doing actually doing calculations again- since

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:24.600
I don’t do a lot of that anymore.. You can
